# The twenty tasks of the P1 protocol's FOURTH round, fixed before the round's
# first cell -- the sieve's included.
#
# WHY A FOURTH SET, AND WHY THEY ARE SHAPED DIFFERENTLY. Round 3 answered its
# question and could not answer it well: `mnema-doc` scored 1.00 on all six
# headline tasks and `mnema+` scored 1.00 on all six, so the subtraction the
# round existed to make was between two saturated arms. A tie at the ceiling is
# not evidence that the two channels are equal; it is evidence that the tasks
# left nothing for a second channel to add. These twenty are written to leave
# room: each axis-A task carries a decision whose APPLICATION to the
# discriminating case is work even with the decision in hand, by one of four
# declared mechanisms, and `sieve.md` says which mechanism each one uses and how
# the ones that still saturate are removed before the comparison.
#
# AND THIS FREEZE IS WEAKER THAN ROUND 3'S IN A NAMED WAY, WHICH IS THE SAME KIND
# OF WEAKNESS ROUND 3 DECLARED ABOUT ITSELF. These tasks were written by somebody
# who knew that round 3 saturated and whose explicit goal was tasks that do not.
# No hash fixes that. Two things are done about it instead of one sentence of
# reassurance: the four mechanisms are declared per task BEFORE any cell runs, so
# "designed for difficulty" is checkable rather than asserted; and the sieve
# measures the difficulty it produced on ONE arm and discards those cells, so the
# comparison is not read on the numbers that selected its own tasks. What no
# procedure here removes is that the author of the tasks wanted them not to
# saturate, and the reader is told that rather than protected from it.
#
# Each hash covers one task directory whole -- its starting repository, its
# ticket, its decision, its two references and its discriminant -- over a
# DETERMINISTIC archive: entry order fixed, timestamps and ownership zeroed,
# Python bytecode excluded. The archive carries the task's id as its path prefix,
# so two tasks cannot swap contents without both hashes moving.
#
# The tasks themselves are withheld until they have been used. Until then this
# file is what a third party can check: not what a task says, but that it did not
# change between the freeze and the result. THE SIEVE USES THEM AND DOES NOT
# PUBLISH THEM: a candidate the sieve drops is a task nothing else has spent, and
# it stays held out.
#
# REPRODUCE -- GNU tar 1.35 and coreutils sha256sum, run from the directory that
# holds the twenty task directories:
#
#   for id in a23-credit-before-tax a24-cycle-anchor a25-late-fee \
#             a26-freight-band a27-payout-cutoff a28-plan-change \
#             a29-exempt-lines a30-retry-budget a31-effective-at \
#             a32-discount-stack a33-branch-registration a34-usage-rollover \
#             a35-dunning-pause a36-credit-note-order a37-sla-clock \
#             a38-reversal-period a39-quota-reset a40-competence-month \
#             b7-roman-numeral b8-run-length; do
#     printf '%s  %s\n' "$(tar --sort=name --format=gnu \
#         --mtime='UTC 1970-01-01' --owner=0 --group=0 --numeric-owner \
#         --exclude='__pycache__' --exclude='*.pyc' \
#         -cf - "$id" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$id"
#   done
#
# A hash nobody knows how to reproduce proves nothing, which is why the command
# is here and not in a report. A different tar implementation will produce a
# different stream: the version below is the one these hashes were taken with,
# and the harness's own `digestOf` was run against these twenty and agreed with
# it hash for hash.
#
